ires $\\frac{2}{7}$ cups of lemon juice for every cup of water. Therefore, the lemonade has a ratio of $\\boxed{}$ cups of lemon juice to $\\boxed{}$ cups of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484230 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "65b04197-0da3-5f1d-9e28-7fa4c1811bab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484230 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's rewrite the fraction $\\frac{2}{7}$ as a ratio:\n\n$$\n2:7\n$$\n\nTherefore, the lemonade has a ratio of $2$ cups of lemon juice to $7$ cups of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484115 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92954" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nJoanna cooks rice for lunch. According to the recipe, the ratio of cups of rice to cups of water is $2:4,$ so $\\boxed{}$ cups of rice are needed for each cup of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6e29d990-90ac-50f9-be81-d4fdf354bbe5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484232 #edb/ref 1074 "We can rewrite the ratio $2:4$ as the following fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the fraction by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{4}=\\frac{2÷2}{4÷2}=\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\frac{1}{2}$ cups of rice are needed for each cup of water." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484255 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484255 #edb/ref 1069 "q-92937" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484255 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484255 #edb/ref 1071 "What is missing in the following sentence?\n\nJason bought $2$ kilograms of almonds and $3$ kilograms of peanuts, so there are $\\boxed{}$ kilograms of almonds for each kilogram of peanuts." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484255 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98f6bb07-5c0b-539a-985d-169696ff401a"